<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bara Sona Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1526</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Great Golden Mosque, Baro Shona Masjid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bara Sona Mosque, the Great Golden Mosque, is a ruined mosque at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the largest surviving monument of the city. Begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a> and completed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-nusrat-shah">Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah</a> in 1526, it is built of brick faced with stone and once had gilded domes, from which its name comes. Its prayer hall, with eleven arched entrances, now stands roofless.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bara-sona-mosque.jpg" alt="Bara Sona Mosque" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SAHABUDDIN MOMIN · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BARODUARI_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
